Categories: transitive-include, implementation-specific, pre-c++26.
1. src/tbb/misc.h uses cpu_set_t without <sched.h>, and two tests call pthread_key_create without
<pthread.h> (libstdc++ includes <pthread.h>, hence <sched.h>, from <thread>, <mutex> and
<atomic>); dynamic_link.cpp and the tests' utils_concurrency_limit.h use POSIX's PATH_MAX without
<limits.h> (libstdc++'s <climits> includes the C library's <limits.h>, which has POSIX's macros
too; libycxx's <climits> defines the C standard's macros only); the vendored doctest uses errno
without <cerrno> (declared through <system_error> by libstdc++ and libc++). Adds the includes.
2. detail/_config.h detects std::uncaught_exceptions by the library's vendor macros (__GLIBCXX__,
_LIBCPP_VERSION) and otherwise calls std::uncaught_exception, removed in C++20 (P0619). Also uses
uncaught_exceptions with another library that defines __cpp_lib_uncaught_exceptions.
3. tbbmalloc is linked with the C compiler, so as not to depend on a C++ runtime, assuming that
what it uses of the C++ library (std::this_thread::yield, the -fno-exceptions error path) is all
inline in the headers. libycxx implements those in its static archive (the platform layer,
DECISIONS §18): links tbbmalloc with the C++ compiler, which adds libycxx's archives statically,
so tbbmalloc still needs no shared C++ runtime.

